public class StubConfiguration extends AbstractConfiguration {
    private static final Logger LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StubConfiguration.class);

    private static final String STATUS_GETTER_URL_POSTFIX = "config/public/stubdescriptor";
    private static final String STUB_CONFIG_STATUS_CHANGE_SETTER_URL_POSTFIX = "config/admin/stub/changestatus";
    private static final String STUB_CONFIG_ORDER_CHANGE_SETTER_URL_POSTFIX = "config/admin/stub/changeorder";
    private static final String DROP_STUB_CONFIG_URL_POSTFIX = "config/admin/stub/drop";
    private static final String SAVE_STUB_CONFIG_URL_POSTFIX = "config/admin/stub/save";
    private static final String GROUP_NAME = "groupname";
    private static final String DIRECTION = "direction";
    private static final String NEXT_STATUS = "nextstatus";

    /**
     * Constructor.
     *
     * @param config the Wilma server configuration
     */
    public StubConfiguration(WilmaServiceConfig config) {
        super(config);
    }

    /**
     * Constructor.
     *
     * @param config the Wilma server configuration
     * @param client the Wilma HTTP client
     */
    public StubConfiguration(WilmaServiceConfig config, WilmaHttpClient client) {
        super(config, client);
    }

    /**
     * Gets the stub configuration information.
     *
     * @return stub configuration information in JSONObject
     */
    public JSONObject getStubConfigInformation() {
        LOG.debug("Call stub configuration API.");

        return getterRequest(STATUS_GETTER_URL_POSTFIX);
    }

    /**
     * Sets the status of the given stub group.
     *
     * @param groupName the name of the stub group
     * @param status    the new status
     * @return <tt>true</tt> if the request is successful, otherwise return <tt>false</tt>
     */
    public boolean setStubConfigStatus(String groupName, StubConfigStatus status) {
        LOG.debug("Call stub status setter API with value: {}, for group: {}", status, groupName);

        return setterRequest(STUB_CONFIG_STATUS_CHANGE_SETTER_URL_POSTFIX,
                ImmutableMap.of(GROUP_NAME, groupName, NEXT_STATUS, Boolean.toString(status.getNextStatus())));
    }

    /**
     * Sets the new order of the given stub group.
     *
     * @param groupName the name of the stub group
     * @param order     the new order
     * @return <tt>true</tt> if the request is successful, otherwise return <tt>false</tt>
     */
    public boolean setStubConfigOrder(String groupName, StubConfigOrder order) {
        LOG.debug("Call stub order setter API with value: {}, for group: {}", order, groupName);

        return setterRequest(STUB_CONFIG_ORDER_CHANGE_SETTER_URL_POSTFIX,
                ImmutableMap.of(GROUP_NAME, groupName, DIRECTION, Integer.toString(order.getDirection())));
    }

    /**
     * Drops the given stub group configuration.
     *
     * @param groupName the name of the stub group
     * @return <tt>true</tt> if the request is successful, otherwise return <tt>false</tt>
     */
    public boolean dropStubConfig(String groupName) {
        LOG.debug("Call drop stub configuration API for group: {}", groupName);

        return setterRequest(DROP_STUB_CONFIG_URL_POSTFIX, ImmutableMap.of(GROUP_NAME, groupName));
    }

    /**
     * Drops the all stub configuration.<br>
     * Whichever drop try was unsuccessful then return {@code false} but try to
     * drop the others. The supposed stub configuration information JSON format
     * is the following:<br>
     * <pre>
     * {
     *   "configs": [
     *     {
     *       "sequenceDescriptors": [ { ... } ],
     *       "dialogDescriptors": [ { ... } ],
     *       "groupname": "Default",
     *       "active": "true"
     *     }
     *   ]
     * }
     * </pre>
     *
     * @return <tt>true</tt> if all the stub configuration is dropped
     * successfully (or was empty and nothing to be dropped), otherwise return <tt>false</tt>
     */
    public boolean dropAllStubConfig() {
        LOG.debug("Call drop all stub configuration.");
        boolean droppedAllStubConfig = true;

        JSONObject stubConfig = getStubConfigInformation();
        if ((stubConfig != null) && (stubConfig.length() > 0)) {
            try {
                LOG.debug("Gets stub configs array from all stub configuration JSON.");
                JSONArray configs = stubConfig.getJSONArray("configs");
                for (int i = 0; i < configs.length(); i++) {
                    LOG.debug("Get the stub group name.");
                    String groupName = configs.getJSONObject(i).getString("groupname");

                    droppedAllStubConfig &= dropStubConfig(groupName);
                    LOG.info("Dropped stub configuration: {}", groupName);
                }
            } catch (JSONException e) {
                LOG.error("Error occurred while dropping sub configuration. ", e);
                droppedAllStubConfig = false;
            }
        } else {
            droppedAllStubConfig = false;
        }

        return droppedAllStubConfig;
    }

    /**
     * Save the actual stub configuration.
     *
     * @return <tt>true</tt> if the request is successful, otherwise return <tt>false</tt>
     */
    public boolean persistActualStubConfig() {
        LOG.debug("Call save stub configuration API.");

        return setterRequest(SAVE_STUB_CONFIG_URL_POSTFIX);
    }
}

original_review:
**Summary**

The `StubConfiguration` class is responsible for managing stub configuration commands in the Wilma service. It provides methods to retrieve stub configuration information, set the status and order of stub groups, drop individual or all stub group configurations, and persist the current configuration. The class extends `AbstractConfiguration` and delegates HTTP communication to a `WilmaHttpClient`. Each method corresponds to a specific API endpoint, and the class encapsulates the orchestration of these API calls.

**Review Comments**

---

**1. The `dropAllStubConfig` method is doing too much at once**

The `dropAllStubConfig` method is responsible for orchestrating the deletion of all stub configurations. However, it combines several responsibilities: fetching the configuration, parsing the JSON, iterating over the configs, extracting group names, invoking the drop operation for each, and handling errors. This results in a method that is relatively long and contains multiple levels of logic, including error handling, iteration, and logging.

Evidence:
```java
public boolean dropAllStubConfig() {
    LOG.debug("Call drop all stub configuration.");
    boolean droppedAllStubConfig = true;

    JSONObject stubConfig = getStubConfigInformation();
    if ((stubConfig != null) && (stubConfig.length() > 0)) {
        try {
            LOG.debug("Gets stub configs array from all stub configuration JSON.");
            JSONArray configs = stubConfig.getJSONArray("configs");
            for (int i = 0; i < configs.length(); i++) {
                LOG.debug("Get the stub group name.");
                String groupName = configs.getJSONObject(i).getString("groupname");

                droppedAllStubConfig &= dropStubConfig(groupName);
                LOG.info("Dropped stub configuration: {}", groupName);
            }
        } catch (JSONException e) {
            LOG.error("Error occurred while dropping sub configuration. ", e);
            droppedAllStubConfig = false;
        }
    } else {
        droppedAllStubConfig = false;
    }

    return droppedAllStubConfig;
}
```

Why it matters:
- The method is harder to read and maintain because it mixes concerns (retrieval, parsing, iteration, error handling, and business logic).
- If any part of the logic needs to change (e.g., how group names are extracted, or how errors are handled), the method will become even more complex.
- According to Fowler's advice, methods should be decomposed into smaller, intention-revealing helpers (see [Extract Method](https://refactoring.com/catalog/extractMethod.html)).

Suggestion:
- Extract the logic for parsing the group names from the JSON into a separate private method (e.g., `extractGroupNames(JSONObject stubConfig)`).
- Extract the loop that drops each group into its own method (e.g., `dropStubGroups(List<String> groupNames)`).
- This will make `dropAllStubConfig` a high-level orchestration method, improving readability and maintainability.

---

**2. The class is cohesive and not a passive data holder**

Upon review, the `StubConfiguration` class is not simply a data holder. It encapsulates behavior related to stub configuration management and does not expose its internal state via getters/setters. All methods perform meaningful actions, and the class is not being manipulated in detail by other classes. There is no evidence of the class being a "data class" in the sense described by Fowler.

---

**3. No evidence of misplaced behavior (feature envy) in this class**

All methods in `StubConfiguration` operate on data and behavior that belong to this class or its superclass. There are no methods that appear to be more interested in the internals of another class. The only external objects interacted with are domain objects (`StubConfigStatus`, `StubConfigOrder`) and the HTTP client, but these are used appropriately as collaborators. There is no sign that any method would be better placed elsewhere.

---

**Summary**

The main area for improvement is the `dropAllStubConfig` method, which would benefit from being broken down into smaller, intention-revealing methods. Otherwise, the class is well-structured, cohesive, and does not exhibit issues related to being a data class or exhibiting feature envy.

