	/**
	 * Removes the state listener.
	 *
	 * @param listener the listener
	 */
	void removeStateListener(StateMachineListener<S, E> listener);

}

public class LimitedSizeDataCollection<STORAGE_DATA extends ComparableStorageData> implements SWCollection<STORAGE_DATA> {

    private final HashMap<STORAGE_DATA, LinkedList<STORAGE_DATA>> data;
    private final int limitedSize;
    private volatile boolean writing;
    private volatile boolean reading;

    LimitedSizeDataCollection(int limitedSize) {
        this.data = new HashMap<>();
        this.writing = false;
        this.reading = false;
        this.limitedSize = limitedSize;
    }

    public void finishWriting() {
        writing = false;
    }

    @Override public void writing() {
        writing = true;
    }

    @Override public boolean isWriting() {
        return writing;
    }

    @Override public void finishReading() {
        reading = false;
    }

    @Override public void reading() {
        reading = true;
    }

    @Override public boolean isReading() {
        return reading;
    }

    @Override public int size() {
        return data.size();
    }

    @Override public void clear() {
        data.clear();
    }

    @Override public boolean containsKey(STORAGE_DATA key) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Limited size data collection doesn't support containsKey operation.");
    }

    @Override public STORAGE_DATA get(STORAGE_DATA key) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Limited size data collection doesn't support get operation.");
    }

    @Override public void put(STORAGE_DATA value) {
        LinkedList<STORAGE_DATA> storageDataList = this.data.get(value);
        if (storageDataList == null) {
            storageDataList = new LinkedList<>();
            data.put(value, storageDataList);
        }

        if (storageDataList.size() < limitedSize) {
            storageDataList.add(value);
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < storageDataList.size(); i++) {
            STORAGE_DATA storageData = storageDataList.get(i);
            if (value.compareTo(storageData) <= 0) {
                if (i == 0) {
                    // input value is less than the smallest in top N list, ignore
                } else {
                    // Remove the smallest in top N list
                    // add the current value into the right position
                    storageDataList.add(i, value);
                    storageDataList.removeFirst();
                }
                return;
            }
        }

        // Add the value as biggest in top N list
        storageDataList.addLast(value);
        storageDataList.removeFirst();
    }

    @Override public Collection<STORAGE_DATA> collection() {
        List<STORAGE_DATA> collection = new ArrayList<>();
        data.values().forEach(e -> e.forEach(collection::add));
        return collection;
    }
}

original_review:
**Summary**

`LimitedSizeDataCollection` is a generic collection class that manages a mapping from a key (of type `STORAGE_DATA`, which extends `ComparableStorageData`) to a list of values of the same type, with each list limited in size. The class provides methods for writing and reading state management, as well as for adding elements (`put`), clearing, and retrieving all stored elements as a flat collection. Notably, it does not support key-based retrieval or containment checks, as indicated by the exceptions thrown in `containsKey` and `get`.

The main responsibility of the class is to maintain, for each unique key, a limited-size, ordered list of values, supporting insertion logic that keeps only the "top N" elements per key according to their natural ordering.

**Review Comments**

---

**1. The `put` Method Is Doing Too Much**

The `put` method is responsible for several distinct tasks: initializing the per-key list if absent, enforcing the size limit, determining the correct insertion point for the new value, and handling the removal of the least desirable element when the limit is exceeded. The method contains a non-trivial loop with embedded conditional logic, making it harder to follow at a glance.

For example, the following block:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < storageDataList.size(); i++) {
    STORAGE_DATA storageData = storageDataList.get(i);
    if (value.compareTo(storageData) <= 0) {
        if (i == 0) {
            // input value is less than the smallest in top N list, ignore
        } else {
            // Remove the smallest in top N list
            // add the current value into the right position
            storageDataList.add(i, value);
            storageDataList.removeFirst();
        }
        return;
    }
}
```

performs both the search for the insertion point and the update/removal logic in a single loop, with comments explaining the intent. This is a classic sign that the method could benefit from decomposition: extracting the logic for finding the insertion index, and separating the logic for updating the list, would make the code easier to understand and maintain. As Martin Fowler suggests, whenever a block of code requires a comment to explain its purpose, it's a good candidate for extraction into a well-named method ([see Extract Method](https://refactoring.com/catalog/extractMethod.html)).

**Suggestion:**  
Extract the logic for finding the insertion index and for updating the list into separate private methods. This will clarify the intent of each step and make the `put` method more readable and maintainable.

---

**2. The Class Holds Both Data and Behavior Appropriately**

The class is not a passive data holder; it encapsulates both the data structure and the logic for managing it. All manipulation of the internal state is done through methods on the class itself, and there are no public getters or setters for the internal map or lists. This is a positive sign: the class is not a "data class" in the sense of Fowler's definition, and there is no evidence that logic operating on the data is scattered elsewhere.

No issues found here.

---

**3. No Evidence of Feature Envy**

All methods operate on the internal state of the class, and there is no indication that any method is more interested in the internals of another class than its own. The only external interaction is with the `STORAGE_DATA` type via `compareTo`, which is appropriate for a collection managing ordering.

No issues found here.

---

**4. The `collection` Method Could Be Clarified**

While not a major issue, the `collection` method flattens all the per-key lists into a single collection. The logic is straightforward, but if the class grows in complexity, it may be worth extracting the flattening logic into a helper method for clarity. For now, this is acceptable.

---

**Summary**

The main area for improvement is the complexity of the `put` method, which currently combines several responsibilities and contains embedded comments to explain its logic. Extracting parts of this method into smaller, well-named helpers would improve readability and maintainability. Otherwise, the class is cohesive and does not exhibit data class or feature envy smells.

